<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cetinje</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cetinje is a town in a high karst basin of southern Montenegro and the country's old royal capital. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-crnojevic">Ivan Crnojević</a> moved his seat there in 1482 and founded its monastery, which became the residence of the prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> who led Montenegro until 1852. It was the capital of the principality and kingdom of Montenegro until 1918 and is still the official residence of the Montenegrin president.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cetinje.jpg" alt="Cetinje" width="320" height="208" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: photo by Darko Bulatovic · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Njego%C5%A1ev_mauzolej.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
